  "hook": {
    "question": "What exactly does an Angular error boundary hand a page-side reader, on the core the subject runs?",
    "finding": "Two shapes, one hook. `ErrorHandler.onViewError(error, details)` receives `{declarationInstance, declarationType, caughtBy}` from the change-detection catch, and `details.boundary = {type, reset}` is added by the boundary's own ON_ERROR hook immediately before the call; the construction catch passes `{declarationInstance, declarationType, boundary}` with no `caughtBy`. `details.declarationType` is the view whose template threw, `details.boundary.type` is the component that declares the boundary - two different LViews, so two different answers. `ng.getContext()` on a node inside the rendered `@error` view answers `{$error, $reset}`, which is the only after-the-fact reader. The rendered fallback is content, never the error: S10 measured the `@error` view carrying `caught: ` and the error's own message in its own text, and a surface that quotes it would be reporting a template, not the throw.",
